// Practice Test 1 — Scenarios: Customer Support (1), Code Generation (2), Multi-Agent Research (3), Structured Extraction (6)
// ============================ QUESTION BANK FORMAT ============================
// To add a bank: copy this file to q4.js (then q5.js, ...) next to index.html. It is auto-discovered — no code changes.
// Keep file numbers consecutive (the loader stops after two missing numbers). Each bank needs a unique `id`.
//
//   { s: 1,            // scenario 1–6 (groups questions like the real exam; names live in js/tests.js)
//     d: 2,            // DOMAIN 1–5 — drives the "Domain accuracy" panel and drills:
//                      //   1 Agentic Architecture & Orchestration   2 Tool Design & MCP Integration
//                      //   3 Claude Code Configuration & Workflows  4 Prompt Engineering & Structured Output
//                      //   5 Context Management & Reliability
//     q: "Question text",
//     o: ["correct", "wrong", "wrong", "wrong"],   // correct option(s) FIRST; shuffled at runtime; keep all options the same length
//     a: [0],          // indexes (into o) of the correct option(s): [0] single answer, [0,1] = "select two"
//     e: "Explanation shown after answering." }
// ==============================================================================
// Authoring convention: the correct option(s) are listed first; the app shuffles options at runtime.
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 1,
  title: "Practice Test 1",
  questions: [
// ---------------- Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent ----------------
{s:1,d:1,q:"Your support agent occasionally stops mid-task: it says “Let me look up that order” and the conversation ends without the lookup happening. The loop code currently exits as soon as the response contains any text block. What change fixes this?",
o:["Continue the loop while stop_reason is “tool_use” and exit when it is “end_turn”, regardless of any text blocks present",
"Keep the text check but add a phrase detector that only stops when the reply contains wording like “anything else I can help”",
"Replace the text check with a fixed cap of ten iterations so that every conversation receives the same amount of tool work",
"Ask the model to append a DONE token to its final message and terminate the loop only after that token has been detected"],
a:[0],e:"stop_reason is the authoritative loop signal: “tool_use” means execute the tool and continue; “end_turn” means the model is finished. A response can contain text AND a tool_use block, so checking for text is an anti-pattern. Phrase detection and sentinel tokens parse natural language (unreliable), and an iteration cap as the primary stop mechanism is an explicitly named anti-pattern."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"After executing lookup_order, your code sends the next API request containing only a user message with the tool_result. The model now responds as if it has no idea what the customer originally asked. What is the correct way to continue the loop?",
o:["Append the assistant's tool_use turn and a user turn containing the matching tool_result block to the running message history",
"Send only the newest tool_result block in a fresh request so the model focuses on the latest information it has received",
"Summarize the tool output into the system prompt and restart the conversation with only the customer's original message",
"Store the tool result in an external cache and instruct the model to request it again whenever it believes it is needed"],
a:[0],e:"The Messages API is stateless. Each iteration must resend the full history: prior turns, the assistant message containing tool_use, and a user message with a tool_result (matching tool_use_id). That's how the model reasons about the next action with the new information. Sending only the latest result drops all context."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Policy says refunds over $500 must go to a human. The system prompt states this clearly, yet audits show about 3% of refunds above $500 are still processed by the agent. What is the most appropriate fix?",
o:["Add a tool-call interception hook that blocks process_refund above $500 and routes the case into the human escalation flow",
"Restate the $500 limit in capital letters at both the start and the end of the system prompt to make the rule more salient",
"Add few-shot examples of refunds over $500 being escalated so the model learns to follow the threshold more consistently",
"Lower the model temperature to zero so that refund decisions become deterministic and always follow the written policy"],
a:[0],e:"When a business rule requires guaranteed compliance, use a hook (e.g., PreToolUse on process_refund) for a deterministic guarantee. Prompt emphasis and few-shot examples are probabilistic and keep a non-zero failure rate. Temperature 0 doesn't make policy adherence guaranteed."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Your MCP tools return dates in different formats (Unix epoch seconds from billing, ISO 8601 from orders) and order status as numeric codes (3 = shipped). The agent sometimes misreads them, telling customers the wrong delivery dates. What is the most reliable fix?",
o:["Use a PostToolUse hook to convert timestamps and status codes into one consistent format before the model ever sees them",
"Add system prompt guidance explaining every timestamp and status code format so the model can convert them on its own",
"Ask each backend team to change their API output format, and pause the agent rollout until all services are consistent",
"Add a separate convert_format tool that the agent can call whenever it notices a value that looks like it is malformed"],
a:[0],e:"PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results and transform them before the model processes them—exactly the pattern for normalizing heterogeneous data (Unix timestamps, ISO 8601, numeric codes). Prompt guidance is probabilistic, re-engineering every backend is disproportionate, and a conversion tool relies on the model noticing the problem."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer writes: “I need to update my shipping address. I want to talk to a real person, not a bot.” The address change is a simple task the agent can do. What should the agent do?",
o:["Honor the request immediately by escalating to a human, passing a structured summary of the customer's details and issue",
"Complete the simple address change first, since it is quick, and then offer to connect the customer to a human afterward",
"Explain that the issue is simple and the agent can resolve it, and only escalate if the customer repeats the request twice",
"Run a sentiment check on the message and escalate only if the frustration score is above the configured escalation level"],
a:[0],e:"An explicit request for a human is an escalation trigger that should be honored immediately, without first attempting investigation or resolution. “Offer to resolve, escalate if they reiterate” applies to frustration WITHOUT an explicit human request. Sentiment scores are an unreliable proxy."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer writes: “This is ridiculous, my package is late AGAIN!” They have not asked for a human. Late-delivery compensation is fully covered by policy and within the agent's tools. What is the best response pattern?",
o:["Acknowledge the frustration, offer the standard resolution for late deliveries, and escalate if the customer asks for a human",
"Escalate to a human immediately, since strongly negative sentiment is a reliable signal that the case is too complex to solve",
"Ignore the emotional language entirely and respond only with the order status details returned by the lookup_order tool call",
"Ask the customer to rate their frustration from 1 to 10 and escalate the conversation automatically when the score is above 7"],
a:[0],e:"Frustration alone isn't an escalation trigger when the issue is within the agent's capability. Acknowledge it, offer resolution, and escalate only if the customer reiterates a preference for a human. Sentiment does not correlate with case complexity."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer asks the agent to match a lower price they found at a competitor. Your policy documents only cover price adjustments when your own site lowers a price. What should the agent do?",
o:["Escalate to a human, because the policy covers only own-site price adjustments and is silent on matching competitor prices",
"Apply the own-site price adjustment rule to the competitor price, since it is the closest policy and serves the customer well",
"Decline the request outright, because any request that is not explicitly permitted by the written policy must be prohibited",
"Process the price match if the difference is under $20, because small amounts carry little financial risk for the company"],
a:[0],e:"When policy is ambiguous or silent on the specific request (a policy gap), the right move is escalation. The agent shouldn't invent policy by analogy, refuse based on an assumed rule, or make up thresholds."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"get_customer returns three accounts for the name “John Smith.” The customer wants a refund on a recent order. What should the agent do next?",
o:["Ask the customer for an additional identifier such as email, phone, or order number before proceeding with any account action",
"Select the account with the most recent order activity, since it is statistically most likely to belong to the current caller",
"Proceed using the first match returned, because the backend sorts results by relevance and the top result is usually correct",
"Merge information from all three matching accounts so that the agent has the complete picture before deciding on next steps"],
a:[0],e:"Multiple matches require clarification (ask for more identifiers) rather than heuristic selection. Picking by recency or rank risks acting on the wrong account—especially dangerous before a refund."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Human agents who receive escalations complain that they must re-ask customers everything, because they cannot see the AI conversation transcript. What should the escalation include?",
o:["Have the agent compile a structured handoff with customer ID, root cause, amounts involved, and a recommended next action",
"Forward the full raw conversation transcript, including every tool call and result, so that nothing is lost in the handoff",
"Have the agent write a short free-text note describing the customer's mood and the general topic of their support request",
"Train human agents to re-verify everything with the customer, since the AI agent's investigation cannot be fully trusted"],
a:[0],e:"Structured handoff protocols (customer ID, root cause analysis, refund amount, recommended action) give human agents what they need when they lack transcript access. A raw transcript dump is verbose and slow to act on; a vague mood note omits the facts; re-verifying everything wastes the agent's work."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"process_refund returns the string “Operation failed” for every problem—timeouts, invalid amounts, and policy violations alike. The agent keeps retrying refunds that violate policy. What is the best fix?",
o:["Return structured errors with an errorCategory, an isRetryable flag, and a readable explanation that the agent can act upon",
"Add a system prompt rule that limits the agent to three retries on any failed tool call before it escalates to a human agent",
"Wrap every tool in automatic retry logic with exponential backoff so that the agent never sees a failed operation directly",
"Return a more detailed but still generic error message that includes the full stack trace from the backend refund service"],
a:[0],e:"Uniform error messages prevent appropriate recovery decisions. Structured metadata (errorCategory: transient/validation/business/permission, isRetryable, human-readable description) tells the agent whether to retry, fix input, explain to the customer, or escalate. Blind retries waste attempts on non-retryable errors."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"A refund request fails because the order is outside the 90-day return window. What should the MCP tool return so the agent handles this well?",
o:["isError: true with a business error category, isRetryable: false, and a customer-friendly note on the 90-day policy",
"isError: true with a transient error category and isRetryable: true so the agent will attempt the refund a few more times",
"isError: false with an empty result body, so the agent treats the refund as completed and moves on with the conversation",
"isError: true with only the raw HTTP 422 status code, leaving the agent to infer the cause from the code by itself alone"],
a:[0],e:"Business-rule violations are not retryable. Flagging isError, categorizing as a business error, setting retryable false, and including a customer-friendly explanation lets the agent communicate the policy accurately instead of retrying or reporting false success."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"lookup_order returns an empty array both when no order exists and when the order database times out. During outages, the agent tells customers “I can't find any order with that number.” What is the right fix?",
o:["Return the timeout as an isError transient failure, and reserve the empty result for successful queries that found no match",
"Instruct the agent to always retry an empty result three times before telling the customer that their order cannot be found",
"Have the agent escalate every empty result to a human, since it cannot tell whether the order lookup actually succeeded",
"Increase the database timeout to sixty seconds so that the lookup rarely times out and the empty results become reliable"],
a:[0],e:"Access failures (timeouts) and valid empty results (successful query, no matches) must be distinguishable. Returning a failure as an empty success silently suppresses the error. Fix the signal at the source rather than working around ambiguity in the agent."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"In long conversations, older turns get summarized. Customers increasingly complain that the agent “forgot” details like “you promised $47.50 back by Friday.” What is the most effective fix?",
o:["Extract amounts, dates, order numbers, and statuses into a persistent case facts block that is kept in every prompt as is",
"Summarize the conversation more frequently so that each summary is shorter and easier for the model to process accurately",
"Increase max_tokens on each request so the model has more room to restate the important figures in each of its responses",
"Instruct the summarizer to be more careful with numbers, and add a reminder that exact figures matter to the customers"],
a:[0],e:"Progressive summarization tends to compress exact numbers, dates, and customer-stated expectations into vague text. Keeping transactional facts in a persistent “case facts” block outside the summarized history preserves them reliably. max_tokens controls output length, not memory."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"Each lookup_order call returns 40+ fields (warehouse codes, internal audit flags, etc.), but return decisions need about five of them. Long sessions are hitting context limits. What should you do?",
o:["Trim each order lookup down to the handful of return-relevant fields before the result is added into the conversation",
"Switch to a model with a larger context window so that the full 40-field order payloads can accumulate without limits",
"Ask the model to ignore irrelevant fields in tool results, since it can focus on what matters when told to do so clearly",
"Call lookup_order less often by caching the first result and reusing it for all subsequent questions in the session"],
a:[0],e:"Tool results accumulate in context and consume tokens out of proportion to their relevance. Trim verbose outputs to relevant fields before they accumulate (e.g., in the tool or a PostToolUse hook). Ignoring fields still costs tokens; caching risks stale data; bigger windows only delay the problem."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"A customer writes one message with three problems: a duplicate charge, a damaged item, and a locked account. The agent resolves the duplicate charge and ignores the rest. What is the best design?",
o:["Decompose the message into distinct issues, investigate each using shared customer context, then reply with one resolution",
"Handle only the first issue mentioned and ask the customer to open new conversations for each of the remaining concerns",
"Escalate any message with more than one issue to a human, because multi-issue cases are too complex for an agent to handle",
"Address the issues in alphabetical order by category so that the agent follows a consistent and predictable sequence each time"],
a:[0],e:"Multi-concern requests should be decomposed into distinct items, investigated (in parallel where possible) using shared context, then synthesized into a unified resolution. Forcing new conversations or escalating all multi-issue messages hurts first-contact resolution."},

// ---------------- Scenario 2: Code Generation with Claude Code ----------------
{s:2,d:3,q:"A new engineer joins and notices Claude Code ignores the team's naming conventions in their sessions, while it follows them for the senior engineer who wrote them. The repository has no CLAUDE.md. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["The standards live in the senior engineer's user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md; move them into the project-level CLAUDE.md",
"The new engineer's Claude Code cache is stale; clear it and restart the session so that the standards reload correctly",
"The project's conventions are too long to be loaded; split them into smaller files so that the model can read them fully",
"The new engineer must run /init in the repository so that Claude Code can regenerate and load the shared team standards"],
a:[0],e:"User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to that user and isn't shared via version control. Team standards belong in project-level CLAUDE.md (root or .claude/CLAUDE.md), committed to the repo. /init would generate a new file from the codebase, not recover someone's personal instructions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Claude Code behaves differently across sessions in the same repo—sometimes following the testing conventions, sometimes not. You want to see which memory files are actually loaded. What should you use?",
o:["Run /memory to see which CLAUDE.md and memory files are loaded in the current session, and open them to edit",
"Run the /compact command, which prints the full list of configuration files that were loaded along with the summary",
"Run claude --debug-config to dump every configuration source and the order in which they were merged at startup",
"Ask Claude in the chat to recite its instructions, since its answer reliably reflects every loaded memory file verbatim"],
a:[0],e:"/memory shows which memory files are loaded and lets you open them—the documented way to diagnose inconsistent behavior across sessions. /compact summarizes the conversation; --debug-config doesn't exist; self-reports aren't reliable verification."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"In a monorepo, the payments package needs the security and API standards files, while the docs package needs only the writing style guide. How should each package's CLAUDE.md be structured?",
o:["Use @import in each package's CLAUDE.md to pull in only the standards files that the package maintainers consider relevant",
"Copy the full text of every standards file into each package's CLAUDE.md so that each package is completely self-contained",
"Put all of the standards into the root CLAUDE.md and rely on Claude to decide which sections apply to the current package",
"Create a slash command per standard and ask developers to remember to run the right ones before they start working on code"],
a:[0],e:"The @import syntax (e.g., @docs/security-standards.md) keeps CLAUDE.md modular and lets each package selectively include relevant standards. Copying duplicates content and drifts; relying on inference is unreliable; manual commands depend on memory."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your root CLAUDE.md has grown to 1,200 lines covering testing, API conventions, deployment, and security. It is hard to maintain and review. What is the recommended restructuring?",
o:["Split it into focused topic files in .claude/rules/, such as testing.md, api-conventions.md, and deployment.md, for clarity",
"Convert the whole file into a single skill so that it only loads when a developer explicitly invokes it with a command",
"Move the content into ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md on each machine so that the project repository no longer carries the large file",
"Delete the sections older than six months, assuming any convention that is still important will be re-added by the team"],
a:[0],e:".claude/rules/ is designed for organizing topic-specific rule files as an alternative to a monolithic CLAUDE.md. A single skill would stop universal standards from always applying; user-level files aren't shared; deleting by age loses valid conventions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You want Terraform conventions to load only when Claude edits files under terraform/, not in every session. What is the correct configuration?",
o:["A .claude/rules/terraform.md file with YAML frontmatter paths: [“terraform/**/*”] so it loads only for matching files",
"A terraform/CLAUDE.md file with a frontmatter field when: editing that tells Claude to load it only during edit sessions",
"A .claude/commands/terraform.md file that developers invoke manually whenever they begin working on infrastructure code",
"An entry in .mcp.json mapping the terraform directory to an infrastructure-conventions server that serves the rules"],
a:[0],e:"Path-scoped rules live in .claude/rules/ with a YAML frontmatter paths field containing glob patterns. They load only when working with matching files, reducing irrelevant context. There is no “when: editing” field; commands are manual; MCP servers aren't a rules mechanism."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your /analyze-codebase skill produces thousands of lines of intermediate output, which fills the main conversation and degrades later answers. What frontmatter change best addresses this?",
o:["Add context: fork to the skill's SKILL.md frontmatter so it runs in an isolated subagent and returns only its result",
"Add allowed-tools: Read to the frontmatter so that the skill can only read files and therefore produces much less output",
"Move the skill into ~/.claude/skills/ so that its output is stored in the user's profile rather than the shared session",
"Add argument-hint to the frontmatter so developers pass narrower arguments that cause the skill to produce less output"],
a:[0],e:"context: fork runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent context, so verbose output doesn't pollute the main conversation. allowed-tools controls tool access, not output volume; location controls sharing scope; argument-hint just prompts for parameters."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your team's /migrate skill needs a table name, but developers often invoke it with no arguments and Claude guesses. Which SKILL.md frontmatter option is designed for this?",
o:["Add argument-hint to the SKILL.md frontmatter so developers are prompted with the expected parameter when invoking it",
"Add context: fork to the frontmatter so the forked subagent can ask the developer for the missing table name itself",
"Add a required: true field to the frontmatter so Claude Code refuses to run the skill whenever arguments are missing",
"Document the parameter in the project CLAUDE.md so Claude remembers to ask for it every time the skill gets invoked"],
a:[0],e:"argument-hint frontmatter tells developers what parameter to supply (e.g., [table-name]) when invoking the skill. context: fork isolates execution; “required: true” is not a skill frontmatter option; CLAUDE.md is always-loaded context, not a parameter prompt."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A /scaffold skill should only create and write files. It must never run shell commands or perform destructive operations. How should you configure it?",
o:["Set allowed-tools in the skill's frontmatter to the file-writing tools it needs, so that shell access is not granted",
"Write “never run shell commands” at the top of the SKILL.md body so that Claude avoids Bash during the skill's run",
"Set context: fork in the frontmatter, since forked skills are sandboxed and cannot run any shell commands by default",
"Place the skill in ~/.claude/skills/ since personal skills are restricted to file operations and cannot use Bash"],
a:[0],e:"allowed-tools in skill frontmatter restricts which tools are available during skill execution—configuration rather than a prompt request. context: fork isolates context but isn't a sandbox; skill location affects sharing, not permissions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A developer wants /review to behave differently for themselves (extra performance checks) without changing the team's shared /review skill. What should they do?",
o:["Create a personal variant in ~/.claude/skills/ under a different name, leaving the team's project skill untouched",
"Edit the project skill in .claude/skills/ directly but avoid committing the change so that teammates never receive it",
"Create a personal skill in ~/.claude/skills/ with the same name so that it silently overrides the team's version",
"Add a conditional block to the shared SKILL.md that checks the developer's username and changes the behavior for them"],
a:[0],e:"Personal customization belongs in ~/.claude/skills/ with a different name, so it doesn't affect teammates or create confusing name collisions. Uncommitted edits to shared files are fragile; per-user conditionals pollute the shared skill."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You have two pieces of guidance: “all TypeScript must use strict mode” and a 12-step release notes workflow used about twice a month. Where should each live?",
o:["Put the strict-mode standard in CLAUDE.md since it always applies, and make release-notes generation an on-demand skill",
"Put both in CLAUDE.md so that they are always loaded and Claude never misses either one when working on the repository",
"Make both into skills so that the context window stays small and developers invoke each when they believe it is needed",
"Put the release-notes workflow in CLAUDE.md, and make strict-mode a skill that the developers invoke before each commit"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md is for always-loaded universal standards; skills are for on-demand, task-specific workflows. Loading a rarely used workflow every session wastes context, and making a universal standard opt-in means it will be missed."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Which of the following tasks is best handled with direct execution rather than plan mode?",
o:["Fixing a null-pointer bug in one function where the stack trace identifies the exact line and the right fix is obvious",
"Migrating a logging library used in 45+ files where several adapter patterns could be applied to the existing calls",
"Choosing between a queue-based and a webhook-based integration that have very different infrastructure requirements",
"Restructuring the authentication module so that it can be shared across three services with different session models"],
a:[0],e:"Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes with a clear fix, like a single-file bug with a clear stack trace. Large migrations, choices between approaches with infrastructure implications, and architectural restructuring are plan-mode tasks."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You need to migrate from one HTTP client library to another across about 60 files. What workflow is most effective?",
o:["Use plan mode to explore usages and design the migration approach, then switch to direct execution to implement it",
"Use plan mode for the whole migration including edits, since plan mode is also the safest mode for making file changes",
"Use direct execution throughout and rely on git to roll back any files that turn out to be migrated in the wrong way",
"Use direct execution for discovery and switch to plan mode only once the edits begin, so that every edit gets reviewed"],
a:[0],e:"The recommended combination is plan mode for investigation and design, then direct execution to implement the agreed approach. Plan mode is for exploring and designing, not making edits; skipping planning on a 60-file migration risks costly rework."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"During a multi-phase refactor, the early discovery phase (searching for usages, reading many files) fills most of the context window before implementation starts. What should you do?",
o:["Delegate the discovery phase to the Explore subagent so verbose search output stays isolated and a summary returns",
"Run the discovery directly in the main session but ask Claude to keep its own responses short to save context space",
"Disable the Grep and Glob tools so that Claude is forced to reason about the codebase structure without searching it",
"Start a new session for each file under investigation and copy the findings between the sessions manually by hand"],
a:[0],e:"The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns summaries, preserving the main conversation's context for later phases. Shorter replies don't shrink tool output; disabling search cripples discovery; manual copying is error-prone."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You describe in prose how to convert legacy CSV records into a new JSON format, but Claude's output interprets your description differently each time. What is the most effective next step?",
o:["Provide two or three concrete input and output examples showing exactly how the sample records should be transformed",
"Rewrite the prose description to be twice as long, with more adjectives explaining the desired transformation behavior",
"Ask Claude to try the transformation several times and pick whichever output version looks the most correct to you",
"Switch to plan mode so that Claude writes a detailed plan of the transformation before any code is actually generated"],
a:[0],e:"Concrete input/output examples are the most effective way to communicate transformations when prose is interpreted inconsistently. More prose keeps the ambiguity; cherry-picking isn't repeatable; plan mode doesn't resolve an unclear spec."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You need to add a caching layer to a service, but you are unfamiliar with caching tradeoffs. You worry you'll miss important design considerations. What technique fits best?",
o:["Ask Claude to interview you about invalidation, failure modes, and constraints before it implements anything at all",
"Ask Claude to implement the most common caching approach first, then review the code for problems once it is completed",
"Write a complete specification on your own first and only then share it with Claude, without any back-and-forth questions",
"Ask Claude to generate five full implementations in parallel and pick the one whose test results look the most promising"],
a:[0],e:"The interview pattern has Claude ask questions that surface considerations you may not have anticipated (cache invalidation, failure modes) before implementation. The other options either skip design discovery or depend on knowledge you don't yet have."},

// ---------------- Scenario 3: Multi-Agent Research System ----------------
{s:3,d:1,q:"The coordinator gathers web results and document analyses, then invokes the synthesis subagent with the prompt “Synthesize the findings into a report.” The resulting reports are generic and miss most of the findings. What is the root cause?",
o:["Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's history, so the complete findings must be included in the synthesis prompt",
"The synthesis subagent's context window is too small, so it should be switched to a model with a larger context window",
"The search subagent did not persist its results to shared memory, which all subagents read automatically when invoked",
"The coordinator's system prompt is too long, so the synthesis subagent is truncating the inherited conversation history"],
a:[0],e:"Subagents operate with isolated context. They don't automatically inherit the coordinator's history or share memory. Everything the synthesis agent needs (search results, analysis outputs) must be passed explicitly in its prompt."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Your coordinator never delegates. Logs show no Task tool calls, and the coordinator does all the research itself with the tools it has. AgentDefinitions for each subagent are configured correctly. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["The coordinator's allowedTools does not include “Task”, so it cannot spawn subagents and falls back to doing work itself",
"The subagent descriptions are too long, so the coordinator decides that the delegation overhead is not worth the effort",
"Subagents can only be spawned from the CLI, not from the SDK, so the coordinator must invoke them through a shell command",
"The coordinator's max_tokens is set too low, which prevents it from producing the larger responses that delegation needs"],
a:[0],e:"The Task tool is the mechanism for spawning subagents, and the coordinator's allowedTools must include “Task.” Without it, the coordinator has no way to delegate. Subagents are fully supported in the SDK."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Research tasks take too long. Logs show the coordinator invokes the web search subagent, waits, invokes the document analysis subagent, waits, and so on—even though these subtasks are independent. What change reduces latency most?",
o:["Have the coordinator emit multiple Task tool calls in a single response so independent subagents run in parallel",
"Have each subagent spawn the next one when it finishes, creating a chain that removes the coordinator from the loop",
"Merge all subagents into a single general-purpose agent with every tool, so that only one agent run is ever required",
"Raise the coordinator's temperature so it is more willing to try delegating more than one task during a single turn"],
a:[0],e:"Parallel subagents are spawned by emitting multiple Task tool calls in a single coordinator response rather than across separate turns. Chaining is still sequential and loses coordinator control; a single mega-agent loses specialization."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"The search subagent passes findings to synthesis as a long prose summary. Final reports contain accurate claims, but citations are missing or attached to the wrong sources. How should context be passed?",
o:["Pass findings as structured records that keep each claim separate from its source URL, document name, and page number",
"Pass findings as one long prose summary and ask the synthesis agent to infer the sources from the writing style used",
"Pass only the source URLs and have the synthesis agent re-fetch every document so it can build citations from scratch",
"Pass findings in markdown with footnotes and trust that the synthesis agent keeps the footnote numbering consistent"],
a:[0],e:"Structured formats that separate content from metadata (source URL, document name, page number) preserve attribution between agents. Prose compression loses claim-source mappings; re-fetching duplicates work; footnote numbering is fragile."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Your coordinator's prompt to each subagent is a rigid 20-step procedure. When subagents hit unexpected source types (e.g., a dataset instead of an article), they fail or produce poor results. What is the best improvement?",
o:["Rewrite the prompts around research goals and quality criteria rather than as rigid step-by-step procedures",
"Add even more procedural steps that cover the unexpected source types so that every possible case is spelled out",
"Remove the prompts entirely and rely on each subagent's description field to tell it how it should do the research",
"Replace the subagents with fixed scripts, since a procedural workflow is better expressed as code than as a prompt"],
a:[0],e:"Coordinator prompts should specify goals and quality criteria rather than step-by-step procedures, so subagents can adapt to what they encounter. Adding steps can't anticipate every case; descriptions are for selection, not task instructions."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Simple factual queries (e.g., “When was the EU AI Act passed?”) go through all four subagents and take three minutes. Complex queries need the full pipeline. What is the best design change?",
o:["Have the coordinator assess each query and invoke only the subagents it needs, not the full pipeline every time",
"Remove the document analysis and report agents completely, since most of the queries do not appear to require them",
"Run the full pipeline anyway but with a cheaper model for each subagent, to bring the cost of simple queries down",
"Add a cache of previous reports and return the closest match whenever a simple query arrives at the coordinator"],
a:[0],e:"Coordinators should analyze query requirements and dynamically select which subagents to invoke based on complexity, rather than always routing through the full pipeline. Removing agents breaks complex queries; cheaper models don't fix latency."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Reports are coherent but sometimes thin on important subtopics. You want the system itself to detect and fill these gaps before delivering the report. What pattern should you implement?",
o:["Have the coordinator check synthesis for gaps, re-delegate targeted searches, then re-run synthesis until it is complete",
"Instruct the synthesis agent to fill any gaps using its own background knowledge so that the report is always complete",
"Run the entire pipeline three times from scratch and merge all three reports together into one final combined report",
"Ask the report agent to add a disclaimer listing the topics that it suspects might be missing from the final report"],
a:[0],e:"An iterative refinement loop: the coordinator evaluates synthesis output for gaps, re-delegates targeted queries to search and analysis, and re-invokes synthesis until coverage is sufficient. Filling gaps from background knowledge loses provenance; reruns are wasteful."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"You run two web search subagents in parallel on the same topic. About 70% of the sources they return overlap, wasting time and tokens. What is the best fix?",
o:["Assign each search subagent a distinct subtopic or source type so that their research scopes no longer overlap at all",
"Let both subagents search freely and have the synthesis agent remove duplicate sources when it combines the findings later",
"Reduce to a single search subagent with a higher result limit so that duplicate sources can never appear between agents",
"Have the subagents share a memory store and check it before each search, so they skip sources the other already found"],
a:[0],e:"Partition research scope across subagents (distinct subtopics or source types) to minimize duplication. Deduping later still wastes the work; one agent loses parallelism; subagents don't share memory by default, so coordination belongs with the coordinator."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"To cut latency, a teammate proposes letting subagents message each other directly instead of routing everything through the coordinator. What is the strongest argument for keeping the current design?",
o:["Keep routing all communication through the coordinator for observability, consistent error handling, and control",
"Allow direct subagent-to-subagent messaging, since removing the coordinator from the loop always improves reliability",
"Let subagents communicate directly but log every message, which provides the same guarantees as coordinator routing",
"Replace the coordinator with a shared message bus that every subagent can publish to and read from without restriction"],
a:[0],e:"Hub-and-spoke: the coordinator manages all inter-subagent communication, error handling, and information routing. That gives observability, consistent error handling, and controlled information flow. Logging alone doesn't provide centralized control or recovery."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Two credible sources report different figures for the same metric: one says 23% market growth, the other says 31%. Both use similar time periods. How should the system handle this?",
o:["Include both values with their source attribution and annotate the conflict, rather than choosing one of them arbitrarily",
"Report the average of the two values, since averaging credible sources tends to produce the most accurate overall estimate",
"Report the value from the more recently retrieved source and drop the other, since newer retrievals are more reliable",
"Omit the statistic entirely from the report, since conflicting values indicate that neither source can really be trusted"],
a:[0],e:"Conflicting statistics from credible sources should be preserved with attribution and explicitly annotated, not resolved by arbitrary selection, averaging, or omission. Retrieval time says nothing about reliability."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The synthesis agent reports that two sources “contradict each other” on smartphone adoption rates. On inspection, one source is from 2019 and the other from 2024. How do you prevent this misinterpretation?",
o:["Require subagents to include publication or data collection dates in their outputs so differences are read correctly",
"Instruct the synthesis agent to always prefer whichever statistic is larger, since larger figures reflect recent growth",
"Filter out all sources older than one year so that the system only ever compares data drawn from the same time period",
"Flag every numerical difference as a contradiction and send all of them to a human reviewer before the report goes out"],
a:[0],e:"Requiring publication/collection dates in structured outputs prevents temporal differences from being misread as contradictions. Preferring larger numbers is arbitrary; blanket age filters discard valuable trend data; flagging everything overloads reviewers."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The document analysis subagent is assigned 10 documents. It analyzes 8 successfully, but 2 return access-denied errors. What should it return to the coordinator?",
o:["Return the eight successful analyses plus structured error details for the two failures, including what was attempted",
"Return a failure for the whole task, because an incomplete analysis could mislead the synthesis agent further downstream",
"Return the eight successful analyses as the complete result set, since the two failed documents are probably not important",
"Retry the two failed documents indefinitely until access is granted, without returning anything to the coordinator yet"],
a:[0],e:"Return partial results along with structured error context (failure type, what was attempted) so the coordinator can decide how to recover. Failing everything wastes good work; silently dropping failures hides gaps; access-denied isn't transient, so endless retry is wrong."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The web search subagent occasionally hits a single HTTP 503 from the search provider, which usually succeeds on a quick retry. How should this be handled?",
o:["Retry the transient failure locally in the subagent, and only propagate to the coordinator if it cannot be resolved",
"Immediately propagate every failure to the coordinator, since the coordinator must make every single retry decision",
"Silently skip the failed search and continue, since a single failure is unlikely to affect the final report's quality",
"Terminate the subagent and have the coordinator restart the whole research pipeline from the very first step again"],
a:[0],e:"Subagents should implement local recovery for transient failures and only propagate errors they can't resolve, including what was attempted and any partial results. Silent skipping hides gaps; restarting everything is disproportionate."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"During a research run, one key database of academic papers was unavailable for the entire session. The rest of the research succeeded. How should the final synthesis reflect this?",
o:["Annotate the synthesis to show which findings are well supported and which topic areas have gaps from unavailable sources",
"Present all findings with equal confidence so that readers are not distracted by technical details of source availability",
"Delay the report until every source becomes available, no matter how long the outage takes to resolve on its own",
"Fill the gaps with the synthesis agent's general knowledge, labeling it as research so the report appears complete"],
a:[0],e:"Synthesis output should include coverage annotations that show which findings are well supported and which topic areas have gaps due to unavailable sources. Hiding gaps misleads readers; filling them with unsourced knowledge breaks provenance."},

{s:3,d:2,q:"Every subagent currently has access to all 18 tools in the system. The synthesis agent frequently launches web searches mid-synthesis instead of working with the findings it was given. What is the best fix?",
o:["Restrict each subagent to the tools its role needs, so the synthesis agent no longer has any web search tools",
"Keep all 18 tools on every agent but add a system prompt rule telling the synthesis agent to avoid using web search",
"Give the synthesis agent even more tools so it has better alternatives available whenever it is tempted to search",
"Rename the web search tools with an internal_ prefix so that the synthesis agent is less inclined to select them"],
a:[0],e:"Too many tools (18 vs. 4–5) degrade selection reliability, and agents with tools outside their specialization tend to misuse them. Scope each subagent's tools to its role. Prompt rules are probabilistic; renaming is a hack."},

// ---------------- Scenario 6: Structured Data Extraction ----------------
{s:6,d:4,q:"You ask Claude to return extracted invoice data as JSON in its text response. About 2% of responses have syntax errors (trailing commas, unescaped quotes) that break the parser. What is the most reliable fix?",
o:["Define an extraction tool whose input schema is the target JSON schema, and read the data from the tool_use block",
"Ask the model to respond with JSON only, and wrap the parsing in a try/except that retries whenever parsing fails",
"Add three examples of correctly formatted JSON to the prompt so that the model copies the exact output structure",
"Post-process the text output with regular expressions that repair common JSON syntax errors before it is parsed"],
a:[0],e:"Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant structured output. It eliminates JSON syntax errors. Retries and regex repair treat symptoms; examples reduce but don't eliminate syntax failures."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your pipeline has three extraction tools (invoice, receipt, contract). Document type is unknown in advance. With tool_choice set to “auto,” the model sometimes replies with prose instead of calling any tool. What should you change?",
o:["Set tool_choice to “any” so the model must call one of the extraction tools but can choose the schema that fits",
"Keep tool_choice “auto” and add a sentence asking the model to please always call one of the extraction tools",
"Force the invoice extraction tool on every request, since invoices are the most common document type in the system",
"Remove all but one extraction tool and merge every field into one large schema that covers all document types"],
a:[0],e:"tool_choice “any” guarantees the model calls a tool (no plain text) while letting it pick which one. That fits multiple schemas with an unknown document type. “auto” allows text; forcing one tool breaks other document types."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Before any enrichment tools run (vendor lookup, currency conversion), extract_metadata must always be called first. How should you guarantee this ordering?",
o:["Set tool_choice to {“type”: “tool”, “name”: “extract_metadata”} on the first call, then handle enrichment after",
"Set tool_choice to “any” and list extract_metadata first, since the model calls tools in the order they are defined",
"Set tool_choice to “auto” and explain in the system prompt that extract_metadata must always be the first tool called",
"Give extract_metadata a name that starts with 01_ so the model sorts it first and selects it ahead of the others"],
a:[0],e:"Forced tool selection ({“type”: “tool”, “name”: …}) guarantees that specific tool is called. Run it first, then process enrichment in follow-up turns. Tool list order and naming don't control selection; prompt instructions are probabilistic."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your schema marks purchase_order_number as required. Many invoices don't have one, and you discover the model invents plausible-looking PO numbers for them. What is the best fix?",
o:["Make the field optional and nullable so that the model can return null when the document simply does not contain it",
"Keep the field required and add a prompt instruction telling the model to never invent a purchase order number value",
"Keep the field required and validate it against a regex so that fabricated numbers with a bad format get rejected",
"Remove the field from the schema entirely and ask downstream teams to look up purchase order numbers by hand instead"],
a:[0],e:"Required fields pressure the model to fabricate values to satisfy the schema. Making fields optional/nullable when source documents may lack the information prevents this. A regex can't catch well-formatted fabrications; removing the field loses real data."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your document_category enum has 8 values. New document types keep appearing, and the model forces them into the closest wrong category. Some documents are genuinely ambiguous. What schema change is best?",
o:["Add an “other” value to the enum with a companion detail string, plus “unclear” for genuinely ambiguous documents",
"Replace the enum with a free-text string field so that the model can describe the category in whatever words it likes",
"Keep the enum as is, since forcing every document into a known category keeps the downstream reporting consistent",
"Add every conceivable category to the enum up front so that the model never encounters a category that is missing"],
a:[0],e:"The recommended pattern is enum + “other” with a detail string for extensibility, and “unclear” for ambiguous cases. Free text loses consistency; forcing wrong categories corrupts data; you can't enumerate every future category."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You use tool_use with a strict JSON schema. Outputs always parse and validate, but sometimes the line items don't sum to the stated total. Why does this happen, and what helps?",
o:["Schemas prevent syntax errors, not semantic ones; add validation comparing the summed line items with the total",
"The schema is not strict enough; adding more required fields will force the model to compute totals correctly",
"Tool use is unreliable for numbers; switch to returning the JSON in a text block and parse it with a JSON library",
"The model is too small for arithmetic; switch to the largest model and the line-item mismatches will stop entirely"],
a:[0],e:"Strict schemas via tool use eliminate syntax errors but not semantic errors (values that don't sum, values in the wrong field). Add semantic validation (e.g., Pydantic) and a retry-with-feedback loop. More required fields or a bigger model don't address the gap."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Validation fails because invoice_date was extracted as “March 4, 2024” but the schema requires YYYY-MM-DD. What should the retry request include?",
o:["The original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation error, so the model can correct its output",
"Only the specific validation error message, since resending the whole document again wastes tokens on each retry",
"The original document alone, with the same prompt, since a fresh attempt is usually enough to fix format problems",
"A generic note saying the output was invalid, so the model re-examines everything instead of only the flagged field"],
a:[0],e:"Retry-with-error-feedback: send the original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation errors so the model can self-correct. Without the document it can't re-check; without the specific error it may repeat the mistake."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Validation fails because contract_end_date is required but empty. You find the end date is stated only in a separate amendment document that was not included in the request. Will a retry with error feedback fix this?",
o:["No; the information is absent from the source, so retries cannot help. Make it nullable or supply the amendment",
"Yes; retrying with the validation error will lead the model to find the date if it looks more carefully this time",
"Yes; retrying at a higher temperature will make the model explore more of the document and locate the date value",
"No; the model cannot read dates, so the date field must be extracted by a separate regex-based parser in future"],
a:[0],e:"Retries work for format and structural errors but are ineffective when the information is simply absent from the provided source. Fix the input (provide the amendment) or the schema (nullable). Pushing harder invites fabrication."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Extraction works well on invoices with line-item tables, but on invoices that describe charges in narrative paragraphs, required fields often come back null. What is the most effective fix?",
o:["Add few-shot examples showing correct extraction from varied formats, such as narrative text versus line-item tables",
"Make every field required so that the model is forced to search harder for the values instead of returning nulls",
"Add an instruction to look carefully everywhere in the document, emphasizing that missing values are unacceptable",
"Preprocess all documents into one standard template by hand before sending them to the model for the extraction step"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples that demonstrate correct extraction from varied document structures address empty/null extraction for fields that are present in an unfamiliar format. Forcing required fields invites fabrication; manual templating doesn't scale."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Some source invoices are internally inconsistent: the printed total doesn't match the sum of the printed line items. Downstream finance needs to know when this happens. What schema design is best?",
o:["Extract both stated_total and calculated_total with a conflict_detected boolean so discrepancies are flagged downstream",
"Extract only the stated_total, since the document's own printed total is the authoritative value for downstream systems",
"Extract only a calculated_total, silently correcting the document so downstream systems always receive a correct sum",
"Reject any invoice whose totals disagree and ask the vendor to resend it before any extraction is attempted on it"],
a:[0],e:"Self-correction validation flows extract calculated_total alongside stated_total and add a conflict_detected boolean for inconsistent source data. That surfaces the discrepancy instead of hiding it in either direction."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You submitted 10,000 documents via the Message Batches API. 140 failed with context-length-exceeded errors. What is the best next step?",
o:["Resubmit only the 140 failed documents, found by custom_id, after chunking them to fit within the context limit",
"Resubmit the entire batch of 10,000 documents, since partial batches make the results difficult to correlate afterward",
"Resubmit the 140 failed documents unchanged, because context-length errors in the batch API are typically transient",
"Move all future processing to the synchronous API, since the batch API cannot handle documents of varying lengths"],
a:[0],e:"Handle batch failures by resubmitting only the failed items (identified by custom_id) with appropriate changes—here, chunking oversized documents. Context-length errors aren't transient, and custom_id makes correlating partial resubmissions straightforward."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Documents arrive continuously and must be fully processed within 30 hours of arrival. The batch API can take up to 24 hours, and your post-processing takes 2 hours. What is the longest interval you can wait between batch submissions?",
o:["Every 4 hours, since a document can wait up to one interval, then 24 hours of batch time plus 2 hours of post-processing",
"Every 6 hours, since 30 hours minus the 24-hour batch processing window leaves six hours to wait for the next submission",
"Once per day, since the batch processing window is 24 hours and submitting more often than that has no benefit at all",
"Every 30 hours, matching the SLA, so that each batch contains exactly one SLA period's worth of incoming documents"],
a:[0],e:"Worst case: a document arrives right after a submission and waits a full interval, then up to 24h batch processing, then 2h post-processing. 30 − 24 − 2 = 4 hours. The 6-hour answer forgets post-processing."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your extraction flow needs the model to call a lookup_vendor tool mid-extraction, receive the result, and then finish the extraction. A teammate wants to move this flow to the Message Batches API for the 50% savings. What is the key issue?",
o:["Batch requests cannot execute tools mid-request and return results, so this multi-turn lookup flow needs another design",
"Batch is ideal here, since each request can call the vendor database tool as many times as it needs within 24 hours",
"Batch works only if the vendor lookup tool is declared with tool_choice “any” so the model must call it in each request",
"Batch is unsuitable only because results come back out of order, which makes the vendor lookups impossible to match"],
a:[0],e:"The batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling within a single request. You can't execute a tool mid-request and return its result. Out-of-order results are handled by custom_id, so that isn't the blocker."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You're about to run a new extraction prompt on 200,000 archived documents via the batch API. What is the most cost-effective approach?",
o:["Refine the prompt on a representative sample first, then submit the full volume to maximize first-pass success",
"Submit the full volume immediately, then refine the prompt based on failures and resubmit until the error rate is low",
"Submit the full volume twice with two different prompts and keep whichever result is better for each separate document",
"Skip prompt testing, since the 50% batch discount makes resubmitting failed documents cheaper than testing up front"],
a:[0],e:"Prompt refinement on a sample set before batch-processing large volumes maximizes first-pass success and reduces iterative resubmission costs. Even at 50% off, re-running large volumes is expensive."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"Your extraction system reports 97% overall accuracy, and leadership wants to reduce human review. Which TWO steps should come first? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Analyze accuracy by document type and field to confirm that performance is consistent across all of the segments",
"Run stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions to measure error rates and spot novel patterns",
"Remove human review for all documents, since 97% accuracy exceeds what most human reviewers achieve in practice",
"Route only documents with self-reported confidence under 99% to humans, without validating that confidence first",
"Reduce review to a random 3% of documents on the assumption that errors are spread evenly across all doc types"],
a:[0,1],e:"Aggregate metrics can mask poor performance on specific document types or fields, so validate by segment first. Stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions measures ongoing error rates and catches novel patterns. Uncalibrated confidence and evenly-distributed-error assumptions are unsafe."}
  ]
});
